# Driving Frame Job from an AI agent (MCP)

Frame Job serves its whole editor as MCP tools, so an AI agent such as
Claude Code can open projects, cut, style, check its work frame by frame and
export, the same way you do in the window. Every edit an agent makes goes
docs/guide/captions.md:304:| `get_transcript` | Reads one clip's words with their timeline times, from the cached transcript or this Mac's recogniser. It never uses the network and edits nothing. Used for cutting by transcript ([Remove Silences and Remove Filler Words](cleanup-tools.md)). |
docs/guide/audio.md:106:- **In Loudness mode it measures through the clip's processing.** A clip with an EQ, hum removal, voice isolation, noise reduction, a gate, a de-esser, a compressor or a voice effect is rendered through them as the export plays it, and its volume is set from what comes out: cleaning up the pauses changes the measured loudness, so the analysis alone would miss the target. With the limiter on, Normalize renders again at the volume it chose and adjusts until the result is within 0.1 LU of the target, at most three times. This takes a moment per clip (0.7 s for a 30-second voice recording), longer on a long one, and a **Measuring Sound** sheet shows "Clip 1 of 2…" and a Cancel button meanwhile. Cancel changes nothing, and if the project changes while it measures, nothing changes and the inspector says so. A clip with none of that processing is measured from the analysis at once, as before.
docs/guide/audio.md:333:The gate turns the clip down in its pauses: when the sound falls below the threshold, it is pushed further down, so room noise and breaths between phrases get quieter while the speech itself is untouched. Its checkbox is labelled **Gate pauses**.
docs/guide/audio.md:355:The leveller evens out a voice that moves on and off the microphone. It works ahead of time: from the clip's measured loudness and the speech found in it, it works out how loud each phrase is and turns each one up or down toward the target, so a phrase starts at its own level rather than catching up after it begins. In the pauses it holds, so the room isn't lifted while nobody speaks, and just before the next phrase (the last quarter of a second of the pause) it moves to that phrase's level. Its checkbox is labelled **Ride the level**. The section is closed until you open it.
docs/guide/audio.md:373:The de-esser tames sibilance — the hard "s", "sh" and "t" sounds a close microphone exaggerates. It listens to the sound above its frequency and, while that band is louder than the threshold, turns only that band down, so the rest of the voice keeps its level. Its checkbox is labelled **Reduce sibilance**.
docs/guide/audio.md:390:The compressor evens out loud and quiet passages by turning down whatever goes above the threshold. Its checkbox is labelled **Smooth volume levels**.
docs/guide/cleanup-tools.md:41:In Voice mode, a clip with no voice in it at all, such as music or a tone, is judged by level instead. The sheet names each such clip, for example "Intro music at 00:00:12:00 holds no voice and is judged by level." That is why, in Voice mode, the threshold row is labelled **Level if no voice:**.
docs/guide/cleanup-tools.md:136:- **A part of the clip.** A `from`/`to` window in timeline time returns only the words that overlap it; a word crossing an edge comes back whole. With a window, the on-device recogniser hears only that part plus 2 seconds on each side. It is faster, but with less context a windowed answer can differ slightly from a whole-clip one. Either end may be given alone: a missing `from` is the clip's start and a missing `to` its end. The window is clamped to the clip and echoed back as `from`/`to`. An empty window, or one outside the clip, is refused.
docs/guide/cleanup-tools.md:142:*Agents:* `get_transcript` (`clip`, optional `language`, `from`, `to`) answers `words` (`text`, `start`, `end`, `filler`, and `speaker` when known), `fillers_reported`, `engine` and, for an on-device answer, `cached`. It edits nothing.
docs/guide/cleanup-tools.md:165:It cuts false starts only when you ask. It can also remove fillers the same way **Remove Filler Words** does, and take out silences the way **Remove Silences** does (`detect_silence`, then one `ripple_delete_ranges`). Any cut an agent makes this way, whether silences, fillers or false starts, is one undo step labelled **Undo Ripple Delete**, not the sheet's name. The assistant can't start a paid filler transcription: press **Transcribe** in the sheet yourself. See [The assistant](assistant.md).
